<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  DEMO-ONLY world for the portfolio video. Copied from worlds/apriltag.sdf
  (the M2+ GATED world -- left completely untouched, no gated number in this
  repo depends on this file) and then made to look better on camera:
    (a) swaps the flat models/apriltag_target board for models/fpv_target
        (same tag, same pose -- wrapped in a primitive "enemy FPV quad" body;
        see models/fpv_target/model.sdf for the byte-identical-tag proof).
    (b) adds a sky + a second fill light + a nicer ground material, so the
        scene isn't flat-dark, WITHOUT touching physics/gravity/ground
        collision/the original sun -- all copied verbatim from apriltag.sdf.
    (c) adds a <gui> block (MinimalScene 3D view + CameraTracking follow +
        VideoRecorder) for a good 3rd-person chase angle and one-click
        recording. apriltag.sdf has no <gui> block and relies on gz-sim's
        own default GUI config when launched non-headless; this world
        intentionally overrides that with a custom one. See "GUI USAGE"
        below.

  WORLD RESOLUTION (same mechanism as apriltag.sdf -- ADR-0005): PX4's
  px4-rc.gzsim resolves world files as ${PX4_GZ_WORLDS}/<name>.sdf, and its
  own gz_env.sh unconditionally overwrites PX4_GZ_WORLDS to PX4's own
  Tools/simulation/gz/worlds directory on every launch. This file must be
  reachable as ${PX4_GZ_WORLDS}/apriltag_demo.sdf at runtime, which means a
  symlink
    ~/PX4-Autopilot/Tools/simulation/gz/worlds/apriltag_demo.sdf -> this file
  (repair with the same one-liner check_m2.sh uses for apriltag.sdf, just
  swap the filename) is needed before launch. Models resolve as usual via
  GZ_SIM_RESOURCE_PATH=/home/emerson/interceptor-sim/models (additive, no
  symlink needed -- this picks up models/fpv_target automatically since it's
  a sibling of models/apriltag_target in that same directory).

  World name is "apriltag_demo" -- distinct from the gated "apriltag" world
  so this world's gz-transport topics (e.g.
  /world/apriltag_demo/model/x500_mono_cam_0/link/camera_link/sensor/imager/image,
  /world/apriltag_demo/pose/info) never collide with a gate run's topics if
  both were ever (mistakenly) running -- they should not be, per ADR-0009's
  "ONE sim at a time" rule, but the distinct name makes a mixup impossible
  to have go undetected either way.

  Launch (mirrors apriltag.sdf's own launch comment):
    PX4_GZ_WORLD=apriltag_demo \
    GZ_SIM_RESOURCE_PATH=/home/emerson/interceptor-sim/models \
    HEADLESS=0 make px4_sitl gz_x500_mono_cam
  (HEADLESS=0 to actually see/record the GUI; use the VideoRecorder plugin
  or gz's own video-recording service for the capture, not a screen
  recorder, so the recording is driven by sim time -- ADR-0009's
  wall-vs-sim-time lesson applies to video capture too, not just scheduling.)

  TARGET ENTITY NAME KEPT AS "apriltag_target" ON PURPOSE: the spawned
  entity below is named "apriltag_target" (matching apriltag.sdf) even
  though it now loads the models/fpv_target model. Every existing mover /
  detector script hardcodes that exact string as the entity to move or read
  pose for -- scripts/m2_detect.py (TAG_MODEL_NAME), scripts/s2_cue_mock.py
  (TAG_MODEL_NAME), scripts/m4_target_mover.py (TARGET_MODEL_NAME), and the
  `gz service ... set_pose` calls in scripts/check_s1.sh, scripts/check_m4.sh,
  scripts/check_s2.sh, scripts/sim_gui.sh, scripts/mc_batch.sh. Keeping the
  entity name unchanged means whatever mission/mover script drives the demo
  flight needs ZERO edits to find/move the target in this world. If the demo
  script is later parameterized with its own target-entity-name flag, this
  can change -- until then, don't rename the entity without updating all of
  the above.

  LIVE-TWEAK KNOBS (marked "TWEAK" below) -- the values most worth adjusting
  by eye once the GUI is up, roughly in the order I'd try them:
    1. <camera_pose> inside the MinimalScene plugin -- the initial static
       chase view; it's a guess (behind-left of the interceptor's start,
       elevated, angled down/toward +X where the target sits). Format is
       "x y z roll pitch yaw" (meters, radians).
    2. CameraTracking follow (see "GUI USAGE" below) for a LIVE chase cam
       instead of the static camera_pose, once the interceptor entity name
       for this run is known.
    3. <sky><clouds><speed> and <time> -- cloud motion / sun height look.
    4. The new "fill_light" direction/diffuse -- how much the shadowed
       (-Y-ish) side of the drones is lit.
    5. ground_plane visual <material> colors -- field tone.
    6. MinimalScene's <ambient_light>/<background_color> -- GUI-view mood,
       independent of the world <scene> ambient/background above it.
  Everything else here (physics, gravity, ground collision, the original
  sunUTC light, spherical_coordinates) is copied verbatim from apriltag.sdf
  -- do not change those for the demo; that's what keeps flight behavior
  identical to the gated runs.

  GUI USAGE:
    - Chase follow: in the 3D view, click the target/interceptor model, then
      right-click it (EntityContextMenuPlugin) and choose Follow; or open
      the Entity Tree panel (docked, collapsed by default -- expand it),
      right-click an entity there, and choose Follow. CameraTracking (the
      plugin backing this) exposes a /gui/track service/topic
      (gz.msgs.CameraTrack) for scripting the same thing headlessly if
      preferred -- run `gz topic -it /gui/track --msgtype gz.msgs.CameraTrack`
      once the sim is up to see the exact fields before relying on it; the
      right-click path needs no such guesswork and is the reliable one.
    - Recording: the VideoRecorder panel has Start/Stop; it's configured
      below with use_sim_time=true (ADR-0009) and lockstep=false (matches
      gz-sim8's own minimal_scene.sdf/underwater.sdf reference examples --
      lockstep=true pauses sim for each frame, which is a bad interaction
      with PX4 SITL's own timing and untested here, so left off).
    - If the 3D view comes up blank (no models/lights visible), the likely
      fix is the one gz-sim's own underwater.sdf PX4 world uses: add an
      explicit list of world-level <plugin> system entries (Physics,
      SceneBroadcaster, Sensors, etc.) before <gui>. apriltag.sdf has none
      and relies on gz-sim's default injection when a world declares zero
      <plugin> elements; adding <gui> is not expected to disable that
      default injection (GUI plugins and server systems are parsed/loaded
      independently), but this world hasn't been run yet -- I did not boot
      Gazebo/PX4 for this task -- so treat that as the first thing to check
      if the scene doesn't populate.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="apriltag_demo">
    <physics type="ode">
      <max_step_size>0.004</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
      <real_time_update_rate>250</real_time_update_rate>
    </physics>
    <gravity>0 0 -9.8</gravity>
    <magnetic_field>6e-06 2.3e-05 -4.2e-05</magnetic_field>
    <atmosphere type="adiabatic"/>

    <!-- TWEAK (3, 6-partner): scene-level mood. ambient/background bumped
         up from apriltag.sdf's flat 0.4/0.7 gray for a less flat-dark look;
         <sky> added. None of this touches the tag's own material (it's
         emissive_map-driven, ADR-0007, so it's independent of scene
         lighting/ambient entirely -- see models/fpv_target/model.sdf). -->
    <scene>
      <grid>false</grid>
      <ambient>0.55 0.55 0.58 1</ambient>
      <background>0.55 0.7 0.85 1</background>
      <shadows>true</shadows>
      <sky>
        <time>10</time>
        <clouds>
          <speed>4</speed>
        </clouds>
      </sky>
    </scene>

    <!-- Ground plane: collision/friction/surface/inertial copied VERBATIM
         from apriltag.sdf -- do not change (takeoff/landing contact
         behavior must stay identical to the gated world). Only the visual
         <material> (TWEAK 5) changed, from flat 0.8 gray to a muted field
         green, for depth/contrast against the sky. -->
    <model name="ground_plane">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <plane>
              <normal>0 0 1</normal>
              <size>1 1</size>
            </plane>
          </geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction>
              <ode/>
            </friction>
            <bounce/>
            <contact/>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <plane>
              <normal>0 0 1</normal>
              <size>500 500</size>
            </plane>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.28 0.32 0.20 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.38 0.22 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 -0 0</pose>
        <inertial>
          <pose>0 0 0 0 -0 0</pose>
          <mass>1</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>1</ixx>
            <ixy>0</ixy>
            <ixz>0</ixz>
            <iyy>1</iyy>
            <iyz>0</iyz>
            <izz>1</izz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
        <enable_wind>false</enable_wind>
      </link>
      <pose>0 0 0 0 -0 0</pose>
      <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    </model>

    <!-- Original sun -- copied verbatim from apriltag.sdf. Do not edit
         (ADR-0007 validated the tag's emissive material specifically
         against this exact sun; even though the tag material no longer
         depends on it for brightness, this light also illuminates the
         ground/drone bodies and there is no reason to disturb it). -->
    <light name="sunUTC" type="directional">
      <pose>0 0 500 0 -0 0</pose>
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <intensity>1</intensity>
      <direction>0.001 0.625 -0.78</direction>
      <diffuse>0.904 0.904 0.904 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.271 0.271 0.271 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>2000</range>
        <linear>0</linear>
        <constant>1</constant>
        <quadratic>0</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
      <spot>
        <inner_angle>0</inner_angle>
        <outer_angle>0</outer_angle>
        <falloff>0</falloff>
      </spot>
    </light>

    <!-- NEW fill light (TWEAK 4): a second, dimmer directional light from a
         different azimuth than sunUTC, purely to soften shadows on the
         drone bodies/ground for camera -- does not cast shadows itself
         (kept soft/non-competing with the sun's shadow map) and does not
         affect the tag (emissive, lighting-independent -- ADR-0007). Safe
         to delete or re-aim; it's additive, nothing else depends on it. -->
    <light name="fill_light" type="directional">
      <pose>0 0 300 0 -0 0</pose>
      <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
      <intensity>1</intensity>
      <direction>0.7 -0.3 -0.4</direction>
      <diffuse>0.35 0.35 0.38 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>2000</range>
        <linear>0</linear>
        <constant>1</constant>
        <quadratic>0</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
    </light>

    <spherical_coordinates>
      <surface_model>EARTH_WGS84</surface_model>
      <world_frame_orientation>ENU</world_frame_orientation>
      <latitude_deg>47.397971057728974</latitude_deg>
      <longitude_deg> 8.546163739800146</longitude_deg>
      <elevation>0</elevation>
    </spherical_coordinates>

    <!-- Demo target: models/fpv_target (dressed-up quad carrying the SAME
         tag), at the SAME pose apriltag.sdf used for the plain board --
         tag center at (5, 0, 0.5), facing -X. Entity kept named
         "apriltag_target" -- see header "TARGET ENTITY NAME KEPT..." above. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://fpv_target</uri>
      <name>apriltag_target</name>
      <pose>5 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- DEMO CHASE/OVERVIEW CAMERA (portfolio-video capture task, added for
         the same reason the world header explains for the GUI's VideoRecorder
         being finicky under WSLg -- a plain camera SENSOR gives a robust,
         headless, scriptable capture path: subscribe to its own gz-transport
         Image topic exactly like the interceptor's onboard mono_cam
         (scripts/m1_capture.py's decode pattern), no GUI needed at all.
         STATIC wide establishing shot (not a live tracking follow -- no
         plugin needed, simplest reliable option): elevated and offset to the
         east/south of the whole engagement corridor (interceptor spawns near
         the world origin; target running-start is out at y~-29, closing
         toward y~0 over the DASH+ENGAGE), pitched down and yawed to look
         back across that corridor. Same camera sensor block style as PX4's
         own mono_cam/model.sdf (RGB_INT8, no distortion) so
         scripts/m1_capture.py's image_msg_to_bgr decode works unchanged.
         hfov 1.7 rad (~97 deg, close to the interceptor's own 1.74 rad lens)
         is wide enough to keep both drones in frame through most of the
         engagement given the pose below; 960x540 (16:9) for video -- NOT
         the original 1280x720 attempt (see below) and not the onboard
         camera's 1280x960 4:3.
         RESOLUTION FINDING (2026-07-07 demo-capture task, logged so it
         isn't re-discovered the hard way): a SECOND full camera sensor in
         this world at 1280x720 measured RTF collapsing to ~0.05 (vs ~1.0
         for the single-camera gated apriltag.sdf under the same idle
         conditions) -- an order of magnitude worse than ADR-0009's
         documented ~0.3-0.5 "under load" floor, enough to make MAVSDK's
         OFFBOARD entry unreliable. Dropping to 960x540 restored idle RTF to
         ~1.0 in isolated (no-Python-client) tests. Do not raise this
         resolution again without re-checking idle RTF first.
         POSE FINDING + STILL UNVERIFIED (same task): the FIRST pose tried,
         `18 -8 14 0 0.667 -2.737` (~24 m from the engagement, pitch ~38 deg
         down), rendered correctly (real content, both drones visible -- see
         the demo capture report's sample frame) but read as a flat, mostly-
         ground, both-drones-tiny wide shot -- not a dramatic "chase" look.
         The pose below is a closer (~14.7 m), shallower-pitch (~30.7 deg)
         REPLACEMENT aimed at the likely DASH/HANDOFF zone (roughly midway
         along the running-start corridor, not the far tag-start point) --
         computed analytically, NOT yet re-rendered/eyeballed (the render
         pipeline wedged mid-task, see the demo capture report's "blocker"
         section). TWEAK: re-verify this pose against a live captured frame
         before trusting it; the four other TWEAK knobs above (lights, sky,
         ground material) are still fair game too. -->
    <model name="demo_chase_cam">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>16 -10 9 0 0.535 -2.820</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <inertial>
          <mass>1</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>0.01</ixx>
            <ixy>0</ixy>
            <ixz>0</ixz>
            <iyy>0.01</iyy>
            <iyz>0</iyz>
            <izz>0.01</izz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
        <sensor name="chase_cam" type="camera">
          <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
          <camera>
            <horizontal_fov>1.7</horizontal_fov>
            <image>
              <width>960</width>
              <height>540</height>
            </image>
            <clip>
              <near>0.1</near>
              <far>3000</far>
            </clip>
          </camera>
          <always_on>1</always_on>
          <update_rate>30</update_rate>
          <visualize>true</visualize>
        </sensor>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ================= GUI (new for this world) =================
         MinimalScene 3D view + the supporting plugins that make its
         interactive features (orbit/pan/zoom, right-click Follow, entity
         tree, video recording) actually work. Plugin set/order follows two
         proven references in this exact environment: gz-sim8's own
         worlds/minimal_scene.sdf (upstream demo of this exact plugin
         combination) and PX4-Autopilot's own worlds/underwater.sdf (a PX4
         world in this repo's own PX4 checkout that already ships a working
         <gui> block). See "GUI USAGE" above for how to drive Follow and
         recording; see the fallback note there if the scene renders blank. -->
    <gui fullscreen="0">

      <!-- TWEAK (1, 6): initial chase-view pose + GUI-view ambient/bg.
           camera_pose is a rough guess: elevated (z=3.5), offset behind and
           to the left of the interceptor's usual start near the origin,
           pitched down ~20 deg and yawed ~35 deg toward +X where the target
           sits (5, 0, 0.5) -- adjust by eye, format "x y z roll pitch yaw". -->
      <plugin filename="MinimalScene" name="3D View">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>3D View</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">docked</property>
        </gz-gui>
        <engine>ogre2</engine>
        <scene>scene</scene>
        <ambient_light>0.55 0.55 0.58</ambient_light>
        <background_color>0.55 0.7 0.85</background_color>
        <camera_pose>-4 -6 3.5 0 0.35 0.6</camera_pose>
        <camera_clip>
          <near>0.25</near>
          <far>25000</far>
        </camera_clip>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Populates the 3D scene with entities -- required for MinimalScene
           to show anything at all. -->
      <plugin filename="GzSceneManager" name="Scene Manager">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Mouse orbit/pan/zoom in the 3D view. -->
      <plugin filename="InteractiveViewControl" name="Interactive view control">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Right-click-in-viewport context menu (Follow, Move To, ...). -->
      <plugin filename="EntityContextMenuPlugin" name="Entity context menu">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Click-to-select support backing the context menu / entity tree. -->
      <plugin filename="SelectEntities" name="Select Entities">
        <gz-gui>
          <anchors target="Select entities">
            <line own="right" target="right"/>
            <line own="top" target="top"/>
          </anchors>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- THE camera-tracking/follow plugin (Follow / Free Look / Look At
           modes; backs the context-menu and entity-tree "Follow" action).
           See "GUI USAGE" above for how to trigger it. -->
      <plugin filename="CameraTracking" name="Camera Tracking">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Entity list (right-click an entity here -> Follow, as an
           alternative to right-clicking in the 3D view). Collapsed by
           default like gz-sim8's own minimal_scene.sdf example. -->
      <plugin filename="EntityTree" name="Entity tree">
        <gz-gui>
          <property type="string" key="state">docked_collapsed</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Play/pause/step from the GUI. -->
      <plugin filename="WorldControl" name="World control">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>World control</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="bool" key="resizable">false</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">72</property>
          <property type="double" key="z">1</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <anchors target="3D View">
            <line own="left" target="left"/>
            <line own="bottom" target="bottom"/>
          </anchors>
        </gz-gui>
        <play_pause>true</play_pause>
        <step>true</step>
        <start_paused>false</start_paused>
        <use_event>true</use_event>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Sim-time / RTF HUD -- handy confirmation this run is being judged
           in sim time, not wall time (ADR-0009). -->
      <plugin filename="WorldStats" name="World stats">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>World stats</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="bool" key="resizable">false</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">110</property>
          <property type="double" key="width">290</property>
          <property type="double" key="z">1</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <anchors target="3D View">
            <line own="right" target="right"/>
            <line own="bottom" target="bottom"/>
          </anchors>
        </gz-gui>
        <sim_time>true</sim_time>
        <real_time>true</real_time>
        <real_time_factor>true</real_time_factor>
        <iterations>true</iterations>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Video recording, available from the GUI panel. use_sim_time=true
           per ADR-0009; lockstep=false (see "GUI USAGE" above for why). -->
      <plugin filename="VideoRecorder" name="VideoRecorder">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="x" type="double">300</property>
          <property key="y" type="double">50</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">50</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">50</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="cardBackground" type="string">#777777</property>
        </gz-gui>
        <record_video>
          <use_sim_time>true</use_sim_time>
          <lockstep>false</lockstep>
          <bitrate>8000000</bitrate>
        </record_video>
      </plugin>

    </gui>

  </world>
</sdf>
